\section*{The interior crack structure of fidelity Mumford–Shah absolute minimizers}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Planar Mumford--Shah interior regularity}: The manuscript claims that the reduced discontinuity set of a planar absolute Mumford--Shah minimizer is locally a smooth arc, a terminating arc, or a junction of three arcs with angle $120$ degrees. In any region with compact closure inside the domain, only finitely many global crack components occur.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For bounded Lipschitz $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^2$ and real $g\in L^\infty(\Omega)$, take relatively closed $K\subset\Omega$ of finite $\mathcal H^1$ measure and $u\in L^2(\Omega)\cap W^{1,2}(\Omega\setminus K)$. Suppose $(u,K)$ minimizes \[\mathcal F_g(u,K;V)=\int_{V\setminus K}|\nabla u|^2+\mathcal H^1(K\cap V)+\int_V|u-g|^2\] against every admissible $(w,L)$ whose change $(L\mathbin{\triangle} K)\cup\operatorname{supp}(w-u)$ is compactly contained in $V\Subset\Omega$, and $K=\operatorname{supp}_\Omega(\mathcal H^1|_K)$. The claimed local crack forms are one embedded $C^{1,\alpha}$ arc through the point, one such arc ending there, or three such arcs meeting only there at $120$-degree angles. Every $U\Subset\Omega$ meets only finitely many global connected components of $K$. The theorem includes the fidelity term and absolute comparison class stated here.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Planar Mumford--Shah interior regularity}: Clipping and Dirichlet comparison retain the given minimizer while putting it into a rectifiable local framework. Blowup classification reduces the unresolved case to a bounded component of a global zero-fidelity limit. Splitting the gradient into a jump-carrying part and an extending harmonic part allows a translated-component comparison that excludes this case, completing the local classification.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Planar Mumford--Shah interior regularity}: The minimizer is absolute and reduced, with bounded fidelity data in a planar domain. The conclusion concerns the entire interior crack set and local finiteness of its global components, not just the regular points known before the classification.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
\textit{Planar Mumford--Shah interior regularity}: The pinned catalogue lists no Lean formalization for this result.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

